.Dd August 18, 2025
.Dt FNF 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fnf
.Nd A general-purpose, interactive fuzzy finder for the terminal
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm fnf
.Op Ar OPTION ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility
reads a list of newline-separated items from standard input and presents an
incremental, interactive selection interface.
As you type a query,
.Nm fnf
scores and filters candidates by fuzzy matching, highlighting matched
characters and instantly updating the sorted list so you can quickly select
the best match.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--separator [=STRING]"
.It Fl 0 , Fl -read-null
Read input delimited by ASCII NUL characters
.It Fl c , Fl -cycle
Enable cyclic scrolling
.It Fl e , Fl -show-matches Ns = Ns Ar QUERY
Non-interactive mode.
Print the matches in sorted order for
.Ar QUERY
to stdout.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print help and exit
.It Fl i , Fl -show-info
Show selection info line
.It Fl j , Fl -workers Ns = Ns Ar NUM
Use
.Ar NUM
workers for searching (default: # of CPUs)
.It Fl l , Fl -lines Ns = Ns Ar NUM
Show only up to
.Ar NUM
results (default: half of terminal height).
Set to
.Ar auto
to use the entire terminal screen.
.It Fl m , Fl -multi
Enable multi-selection
.It Fl M , Fl -max-items Ns = Ns Ar NUM
Load only up to
.Ar NUM
items (default: unlimited)
.It Fl p , Fl -prompt Ns = Ns Ar STRING
Input prompt (default: "> ")
.It Fl P , Fl -padding Ns = Ns Ar NUM
Left pad the list of matches
.Ar NUM
places (default: 0)
.It Fl q , Fl -query Ns = Ns Ar QUERY
Use
.Ar QUERY
as the initial search query
.It Fl r , Fl -reverse
List from top, prompt at bottom
.It Fl s , Fl -show-scores
Show the scores for each item
.It Fl t , Fl -tty Ns = Ns Ar TTY
Use
.Ar TTY
instead of the default tty device
.Pq Pa /dev/tty
.It Fl v , Fl -version
Print version and exit
.It Fl -case Ns = Ns Ar MODE
Set case sensitivity mode to
.Ar MODE
[respect|ignore|smart] (default: smart).
.Pp
In smart mode, the search is case-insensitive by default, but it becomes
case-sensitive if the query contains at least one uppercase character.
.It Fl -color Ns = Ns Ar COLORSPEC
Set custom colors (consult the
.Sx COLORS
section below for more information)
.It Fl -color-scheme Ns = Ns Ar SCHEME
Set the base color scheme to
.Ar SCHEME .
.Pp
Available schemes:
.Cm dark
(default),
.Cm light
(for light 256-color terminals), and
.Cm 16
(for 16-color terminals).
.Pp
Individual colors will be overriden by the values set via the
.Fl -color
option or the environment variable
.Ev FNF_COLORS .
.It Fl -ghost Ns = Ns Ar STR
Text to display when input is empty
.It Fl -height Ns = Ns Ar NUM
Alias for
.Fl -lines
.It Fl -marker Ns = Ns Ar STRING
Multi-select marker (default: '✔' or *', depending on
.Fl -no-unicode )
.It Fl -no-bold
Do not use bold colors
.It Fl -no-clear
Do not clear the interface on exit
.It Fl -no-color
Disable colors
.It Fl -no-sort
Do not sort the result
.It Fl -no-unicode
Disable Unicode decorations
.It Fl -pointer Ns = Ns Ar STRING
Pointer to highlighted match (default: '▌' or '>', depending on
.Fl -no-unicode )
.It Fl -print-null
Print output delimited by ASCII NUL characters
.It Fl -separator Op Ns = Ns Ar STRING
Print a horizontal separator on the info line
.Pq Fl -show-info
is implied.
.Pp
If specified,
.Ar STR
is used to form the separator.
Otherwise, it defaults to
.Dq ─
or
.Dq - ,
depending on
.Fl -no-unicode .
.It Fl -right-accepts
Right arrow key accepts: print selection and exit
.It Fl -tab-accepts
TAB accepts: print selection and exit
.It Fl -left-aborts
Left arrow key aborts: cancel selection and exit
.El
.Sh KEY BINDINGS
.Bl -tag -width "Ctrl+PgDn / Ctrl+PgUp"
.It Cm ENTER
Print the selected items to stdout and exit.
If
.Fl -right-accepts
is set, the Right arrow key performs the same function.
Equally, if
.Fl -tab-accepts
is set, the TAB key performs the same function.
.It Cm "ESC, Ctrl+c, Ctrl+g"
Exit without printing any result.
If
.Fl -left-aborts
is set, the Left arrow key performs the same function.
.It Cm "Up Arrow, Ctrl+p, Ctrl+k"
Select the previous item.
.It Cm "Down Arrow, Ctrl+n, Ctrl+j"
Select the next item.
.It Cm "PgDn / PgUp"
Advance/go-back one page.
.It Cm "Ctrl+PgDn / Ctrl+PgUp"
Jump to the first/last available item.
.It Cm TAB
If multi-selection is enabled
.Pq Fl m , Fl -multi ,
\&(un)mark the selected item and select the next one.
.It Cm "Shift+TAB (btab)"
If multi-selection enabled
.Pq Fl m , Fl -multi ,
\&(un)mark the selected item and select the previous one.
.It Cm "Backspace, Ctrl+h"
Delete the character before the cursor.
.It Cm Del
Delete the character next to the cursor.
.It Cm Ctrl+w
Delete the word before the cursor.
.It Cm Ctrl+u
Delete the entire line.
.It Cm Ctrl+d
Same as
.Cm Del
(aborts
.Nm fnf
if the query is empty).
.El
.Sh COLORS
Color configuration is made using the
.Fl -color
option, which takes a color string composed of one or more comma- or
space-separated fields.
Each field is a color definition with this general format:
.Li COLOR_NAME:ANSI_COLOR[:ATTRIBUTE] .
.Ss COLOR NAMES
Available color names (or interface elements):
.Bl -tag -width separator
.It Cm fg
Item foreground color
.It Cm gutter
Gutter on the left
.It Cm hl
Highlighted substrings in the selected line
.It Cm ghost
Ghost text
.It Cm info
Info line (match counters)
.It Cm marker
Multi-selection marker
.It Cm prompt
Prompt
.It Cm pointer
Pointer to the current line
.It Cm query
Query foreground color
.It Cm score
Item score
.It Cm sel-bg
Selected line background
.It Cm sel-fg
Selected line foreground
.It Cm separator
Horizontal separator on info line
.El
.Ss ANSI COLORS
Colors are defined either as ANSI 16 colors
.Pq Cm 0-15 ,
256 colors
.Pq Cm 16-255 ,
or 24-bit colors
.Pq Cm #RRGGBB .
.Pp
Use
.Cm -1
for the default terminal foreground/background color (or the original color
of the text).
.Ss ATTRIBUTES
A single numbered attribute
.Pq Cm 0-9
can be added to colors.
The meaning of these numbers is this:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm 0
Reset all attributes
.It Cm 1
Bold or increased intensity
.It Cm 2
Faint, decreased intensity or dim
.It Cm 3
Italic (Not widely supported)
.It Cm 4
Underline
.It Cm 5
Slow blink
.It Cm 6
Rapid blink
.It Cm 7
Reverse video or invert
.It Cm 8
Conceal or hide (Not widely supported)
.It Cm 9
Crossed-out or strike
.El
.Ss EXAMPLE
In the line
.Li --color="prompt:214:1,pointer:#87d700:2,marker:6,sel-fg:#ffff00" ,
the prompt is set to bold orange (256-color), the pointer to a dimmed green
(hex color), the marker to cyan (256-color), and the foreground color for
selected items to yellow (hex color).
.Pp
A few color schemes:
.Bl -tag -width light
.It Cm dark
fg:-1,ghost:243,gutter:-1,hl:216,info:144,marker:72,pointer:124,prompt:74:1,query:-1,score:102,sel-bg:236,sel-fg:7:1,separator:240
.It Cm light
fg:-1,ghost:243,gutter:-1,hl:90,info:233,marker:160,pointer:6:2,prompt:21:1,query:-1,score:238,sel-bg:251,sel-fg:0,separator:238
.It Cm 16
fg:-1,ghost:7:2,gutter:-1,hl:6,info:7:2,marker:2,pointer:1:1,prompt:6:1,query:-1,score:7:2,sel-bg:0,sel-fg:7:1,separator:7:2
.It Cm bw
Run with
.Fl -no-color
.El
.Pp
The default color scheme is
.Cm dark .
If a color is not specified in the command line, the default value is used.
For example,
.Li --color=prompt:172
sets the prompt to the specified color, and uses the default values (those in
.Cm dark )
for everything else.
To use a different base color scheme use the
.Fl -color-scheme
option (see above).
.Pp
The
.Ev FNF_COLORS
environment variable can also be used just as with the
.Fl -color
option.
E.g.:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ export FNF_COLORS="prompt:214:1,pointer:#87D700:2,marker:6,sel-fg:#FFFF00"
$ ls | fnf
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Normal exit
.It 1
No match
.It 130
Interrupted with
.Cm Ctrl+c ,
.Cm Ctrl+d ,
.Cm Ctrl+g ,
or
.Cm Esc
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bl -tag -width "ps aux | fnf | awk '{ print $2 }' | xargs kill"
.It Li "ls | fnf"
Present a menu of items in the current directory
.It Li "ls | fnf -l 25"
Same as above, but show 25 lines of items
.It Li "vi \&$(find -type f | fnf)"
List files under the current directory and open the one selected in
.Xr vi 1
.It Li "cd \&$(find -type d | fnf)"
Present all directories under current path, and change to the one selected
.It Li "ps aux | fnf | awk '{ print $2 }' | xargs kill"
List running processes, kill the selected process
.It Li "git checkout \&$(git branch | cut -c 3- | fnf)"
Same as above, but switching git branches
.El
